---
import LongTailCalculator from '../components/LongTailCalculator.astro';

const fields = [
  { id: 'length', label: 'Length (ft)', type: 'number', min: 1, step: 0.5, value: '50' },
  { id: 'width', label: 'Width (ft)', type: 'number', min: 1, step: 0.5, value: '10' },
  { id: 'depth', label: 'Depth (in)', type: 'number', min: 1, step: 0.5, value: '4' },
  { id: 'density', label: 'Stone density (tons/cu yd)', type: 'number', min: 1.1, max: 1.8, step: 0.05, value: '1.4' },
  { id: 'price', label: 'Price ($/ton)', type: 'number', min: 10, step: 5, value: '40' },
  { id: 'waste', label: 'Waste (%)', type: 'number', min: 0, max: 30, step: 1, value: '10' },
];

const results = [
  { key: 'cubicYards', label: 'Gravel volume', unit: 'cu yd', format: 'fixed2' },
  { key: 'tons', label: 'Gravel weight', unit: 'tons', format: 'fixed2' },
  { key: 'materialCost', label: 'Material cost', format: 'money' },
];
---

<LongTailCalculator
  title="Gravel Driveway Cost Calculator"
  description="Free gravel driveway cost calculator: enter size, depth, stone density and price per ton to estimate cubic yards, tons and total material cost."
  slug="gravel-driveway-cost-calculator"
  calcType="gravel-cost"
  quickAnswer="A 10x50 driveway at 4 inches deep needs about 9.5 tons of crushed stone with 10% waste. At $40 per ton, material alone is about $380."
  formula="Volume = length × width × (depth ÷ 12) ÷ 27. Tons = volume × density. Cost = tons × price per ton."
  fields={fields}
  results={results}
  table={{
    caption: 'Gravel driveway material cost at 1.4 tons per cubic yard, $40 per ton, and 10% waste.',
    headers: ['Driveway size', 'Depth', 'Tons', 'Material cost'],
    rows: [
      ['10 x 50', '4"', '9.5', '$380'],
      ['12 x 50', '4"', '11.4', '$456'],
      ['10 x 100', '4"', '19.0', '$760'],
      ['12 x 100', '4"', '22.8', '$912'],
      ['20 x 60', '6"', '30.5', '$1,220'],
      ['20 x 100', '6"', '50.9', '$2,036'],
    ],
  }}
  note="Most suppliers charge delivery separately. A local short-haul delivery is often $50 to $150; longer hauls can exceed $300."
  guide={[
    {
      heading: 'What changes gravel price most',
      paragraphs: [
        'Stone type, distance from the quarry, and delivery minimums matter more than most people expect.',
        'Crushed base stone is usually the cheapest per ton, while pea gravel, river rock, and decorative chip cost more because they are washed and sorted.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'How to order without overpaying',
      paragraphs: [
        'Get a delivered price per ton, not just the yard price. A low material price can disappear under a high delivery fee on a small order.',
        'If your driveway needs more than 10 tons, ask about a full-truck discount. Suppliers often price partial loads higher per ton.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'What a gravel driveway really costs over time',
      paragraphs: [
        'Gravel is one of the cheapest driveways to install, but it needs grading and top-ups.',
        'A well-built driveway with fabric, compacted base, and the right surface stone can last many years with light maintenance. A thin layer over mud will need rebuilding quickly.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'The full cost picture: material, delivery and labor',
      paragraphs: [
        'Material is only the first line. Delivery on a partial load typically runs $50 to $300 depending on haul distance, and a full truckload usually waives part of it.',
        'Spreading and compacting the stone yourself costs nothing but an afternoon and a rented plate compactor, usually $45 to $75 for a day. Hiring a crew to spread and grade adds roughly $30 to $50 per hour and turns a weekend job into a half-day one.',
        'Budget the driveway fabric separately: a 10 x 50 roll of woven geotextile runs $60 to $120 and is the single cheapest way to keep a gravel drive from sinking into the subsoil.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'What each stone type costs per ton',
      paragraphs: [
        'At most quarries and landscape yards, crushed base stone (#411 or #304) lands at the low end of the $30 to $60 range because it is made by crushing, not washing.',
        'Clean surface stone like #57 runs mid-range. Pea gravel, river rock and decorative chips sit at the top of the range, and they are the wrong choice for a base layer anyway: rounded stone never locks into a compacted surface.',
        'Limestone tends to be the cheapest crushed stone in the Midwest and East; granite and trap rock cost more where limestone is not quarried locally.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'How depth choice changes the bill',
      paragraphs: [
        'The table rows above show it directly: the same 20 x 100 driveway costs $2,036 in material at 6 inches versus roughly half that at 4 inches.',
        'The two-layer 6-inch build is not an upgrade to skip where vehicles are concerned — a 4-inch layer under repeated truck loads ruts within a season or two, and re-graveling every year costs more than the extra tons did once.',
      ],
    },
  ]}
  faqs={[
    {
      question: 'How much does a gravel driveway cost per square foot?',
      answer: 'About $0.70 to $1.20 per square foot for material at 4 inches deep and $30 to $60 per ton.',
    },
    {
      question: 'How many tons of gravel do I need for a 10x50 driveway?',
      answer: 'About 8.6 tons net, or 9.5 tons with 10% waste for compaction and spillage.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Why do I need extra gravel?',
      answer: 'Loose gravel compacts and spreads at the edges. A 10% waste margin is normal; 15% is better on soft ground or slopes.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Does gravel type change the cost?',
      answer: 'Yes. Dense base stone is heavier and often cheaper per ton than decorative pea gravel or river rock.',
    },
    {
      question: 'How much does a 20x100 gravel driveway cost?',
      answer: 'At 6 inches deep, 20 x 100 takes about 50.9 tons with waste, which is roughly $2,036 in material at $40 per ton — before delivery. At 4 inches for car-only use, about 34 tons or $1,360.',
    },
    {
      question: 'Is a gravel driveway cheaper than asphalt or concrete?',
      answer: 'Material-wise, far cheaper: gravel runs $0.70 to $1.20 per square foot installed by the owner, while asphalt is typically $3 to $7 and concrete $4 to $8 per square foot professionally installed. Gravel needs more maintenance; pavement needs none for years, which is the trade.',
    },
    {
      question: 'How much gravel do I need for a 2-car driveway?',
      answer: 'A two-car pad is usually 20 x 20 feet. At 4 inches deep that is about 5 cubic yards, or 7.4 tons with waste — roughly $300 of stone at $40 per ton, plus delivery.',
    },
    {
      question: 'What is the cheapest way to buy gravel?',
      answer: 'Buy by the full truckload from the quarry or a local supplier rather than bagged stone at a home center. Bagged gravel runs several times the per-ton price once you need more than a ton; the calculator above only makes sense against delivered bulk pricing.',
    },
  ]}
  related={[
    { label: 'Gravel driveway depth', href: '/gravel-driveway-depth-calculator/' },
    { label: 'Gravel driveway calculator', href: '/gravel-driveway-calculator/' },
    { label: 'Concrete slab cost', href: '/concrete-slab-cost-calculator/' },
  ]}
/>
